  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 215 PM EST Wed Nov 18 2026\n\nPattern Synopsis:\n\nMid/upper-level ridging will continue to slide across the eastern \nhalf of the CONUS through the middle of the week as a strong trough \npunches over the Upper Midwest/Great Lakes. In turn, surface high \npressure will remain locked in for most areas east of the \nMississippi into this weekend as aforementioned troughing provides \nample forcing aloft to support the cyclone over the northern Great \nPlains as it treks into southern Canada. This system will weaken \nwith time through the end of the week as it races across \nOntario/Quebec. Meanwhile, confidence continues to increase in a \nsecond system developing along the remnant cold front draped across \nthe Midwest this weekend as additional troughing works across the \ncentral CONUS, likely moving into the Great Lakes by Sunday morning. \nLooking ahead, expansive high pressure appears to build across the \nGreat Plains and much of the Midwest through the first half of next \nweek.\n\nForecast Details:\n\nPrimary focus remains unchanged as seasonably-mild, rain-free, and \nbreezy conditions are expected across northern Michigan for the \ncoming days. High pressure nosing into the region from the southeast \nwill keep rain chances very low through Saturday, even with a \ncyclone passing just to our north as a relatively dry airmass stays \nin place. Breezy southwest winds will persist across the Great Lakes \nthrough Thursday as the pressure gradient tightens between said high \npressure and passing cyclone. Strongest winds gusting to 25-40 mph \nare most likely on Thursday before diminishing Thursday night into \nFriday. Mild temperatures with highs reaching into the 50s and low \n60s are also anticipated for the next several days -- through \nSaturday at the least, and potentially Sunday as well depending on \nsystem track and timing. These temperatures will be 15-20 degrees \nwarmer than mid/late November averages.\n\nOur next precip chances enter the picture during the second half of \nthe weekend as the aforementioned system and associated cold front \nswing across the Northwoods. Best rain chances are currently \nexpected to be from Sunday morning into Sunday evening, diminishing \nwith time as the system exits the Great Lakes Sunday night. As the \nprevious forecast discussed, uncertainty exists in the \npotential/magnitude of any lake effect snow on the backside of the \ncyclone Sunday night into Monday. Certainly cannot be ruled out with \nwarm lakes this time of year, but a relatively dry airmass quickly \nmoving in will likely put a limit on accumulating snow potential to \nstart next week. Otherwise, high pressure is expected to build in \nearly next week, likely keeping the forecast dry as we head into \nThanksgiving; albeit with high temperatures dipping into the 30s and \novernight lows in the 20s.",
